<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan José Arévalo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan José Arévalo was a Guatemalan educator and philosopher who became the country's first democratically elected president after the revolution of 1944, serving from 1945 to 1951. His government adopted a new constitution and introduced social security, a labour code and autonomy for the University of San Carlos, under a programme he called 'spiritual socialism'. He survived many coup attempts and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-jose-arevalo.jpg" alt="Juan José Arévalo" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Estudio Fleischmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_estudio_del_Dr._Juan_Jos%C3%A9_Ar%C3%A9valo_Bermejo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan José Arévalo Bermejo was born in Taxisco, Santa Rosa, on 10 September 1904, into a lower-middle-class family. He trained as a teacher at the Central Normal School for Boys and, with his friend Luis Martínez Mont, founded a literary magazine, Alba. In 1927 he won a government scholarship to study pedagogy abroad and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, where in 1934 he took a doctorate in philosophy and educational sciences at the National University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-plata">La Plata</a>. In 1932 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a> cut the monthly pension for scholars like him from 175 to 116 quetzales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Arévalo was elected in 1944 in what is generally reckoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s first truly free election, with over 86 percent of the vote, and served from 15 March 1945 to 15 March 1951. His administration saw wide political freedom, a new constitution in 1945, a new minimum wage, new schools, hospitals and houses, and the formation of parties and trade unions. His philosophy, which he called spiritual socialism, aimed at the moral development of Guatemalans. He valued property rights but would subordinate them to the benefit of the country, and he rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, exiling activists and refusing to legalise the Communist Party.</p><p>Even so he faced at least 25 coup attempts, as conservatives and foreign governments read his programme as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>. The benefits of his reforms did not reach the rural areas, which needed an agrarian reform that his party in Congress could not achieve. He handed power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a> in 1951.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Árbenz's fall in 1954 Arévalo went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where in 1956 he wrote the allegory The Shark and the Sardines against the treatment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and American companies. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> on 27 March 1963 to announce his candidacy, but a coup on 31 March put Enrique Peralta Azurdia in power and he fled again. He met President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinicio-cerezo">Vinicio Cerezo</a> in January 1986 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> in October 1990. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-arevalo">Bernardo Arévalo</a> became president of Guatemala in 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan José Arévalo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Juan José Arévalo</b>, president from 1945 to 1951, introduced a new constitution, social security, a labour code and autonomy for the University of San Carlos, and his successor Jacobo Árbenz enacted the agrarian reform of Decree 900 in 1952, which expropriated uncultivated land on large estates, including much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Taxisco, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.07,-90.47&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Taxisco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juan José Arévalo"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4961 3809h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4961 3809h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.07,-90.47&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
